.TH OPENTELEMETRY-JMX-SCRAPER 8 "@DATE@" "opentelemetry-jmx-scraper @VERSION@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
opentelemetry-jmx-scraper \- collect JVM metrics over JMX and export them via OTLP
.SH DESCRIPTION
The OpenTelemetry JMX scraper connects to a configured JMX endpoint and exports metrics through OTLP.
It runs as the systemd service opentelemetry-jmx-scraper.service.
.PP
The scraper needs a Java runtime, which this package does not install.
The runtime is a suggested dependency rather than a required one, so package managers do not pull it in automatically.
Install
.B default-jre
on Debian and Ubuntu, or
.B java-headless
on RPM-based distributions.
.PP
The service unit resolves
.B java
through the PATH of the service, so whichever JVM is first on that PATH is used.
Without one, the service fails to start with "env: java: No such file or directory".
.SH CONFIGURATION
Set otel.jmx.service.url and otel.jmx.target.system in /etc/opentelemetry/jmx-scraper/config.properties before starting the service.
The service reads the optional environment overrides from /etc/opentelemetry/jmx-scraper/jmx-scraper.env.
.TP
.B otel.jmx.service.url
The JMX endpoint to connect to, for example service:jmx:rmi:///jndi/rmi://localhost:9010/jmxrmi.
.TP
.B otel.jmx.target.system
The metric definitions to apply, as one or more comma-separated values from:
.RS
.\" PD 0 sets the inter-paragraph distance to zero, which keeps the bullets
.\" below on consecutive lines instead of double-spaced. The bare .PD after
.\" the list restores the default.
.PD 0
.\" BEGIN GENERATED target systems (make update-jmx-target-systems)
.IP \(bu 2
activemq
.IP \(bu 2
camel
.IP \(bu 2
cassandra
.IP \(bu 2
experimental-cassandra
.IP \(bu 2
experimental-kafka-connect
.IP \(bu 2
hadoop
.IP \(bu 2
hbase
.IP \(bu 2
jetty
.IP \(bu 2
jvm
.IP \(bu 2
kafka
.IP \(bu 2
kafka-consumer
.IP \(bu 2
kafka-producer
.IP \(bu 2
solr
.IP \(bu 2
tomcat
.IP \(bu 2
wildfly
.\" END GENERATED
.PD
.PP
For example, "jvm,tomcat" collects the JVM metrics alongside the Tomcat ones.
That list is what the packaged scraper accepts; see
.UR https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-java-contrib/tree/main/jmx-scraper
.UE
for what each target system collects.
.RE
.TP
.B otel.jmx.remote.registry.ssl
Set this when the target JVM runs with
com.sun.management.jmxremote.registry.ssl=true.
.PP
Credentials are configured through the environment rather than this file; see
ENVIRONMENT and SECURITY.
.PP
The file also accepts the OpenTelemetry Java SDK's autoconfiguration
properties, so otel.exporter.otlp.protocol, otel.exporter.otlp.endpoint,
otel.service.name, and otel.metric.export.interval all work here.
See
.UR https://opentelemetry.io/docs/languages/java/configuration/
.UE
for that set.
.PP
otel.exporter.otlp.headers is the exception, because it is where an OTLP API
key goes. Set OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS in the environment file instead; see
SECURITY.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The service reads these from the optional environment file listed under FILES,
which overrides the defaults the unit sets.
.TP
.B JMX_SCRAPER_CONFIG
Path to the scraper configuration, defaulting to
/etc/opentelemetry/jmx-scraper/config.properties.
.TP
.B JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS
Options the Java 9 and later launcher prepends to the command line. The unit
sets it to
.B \-\-add\-exports=java.security.sasl/com.sun.security.sasl=ALL\-UNNAMED
\(em without it the scraper cannot install the SASL security provider on Java 16
and later, and every SASL remote profile fails to authenticate. Override this
only if that export is kept.
.TP
.B PATH
Where the unit resolves
.B java
from. Set it to put a specific JVM first when the host has several, or when the
JVM to use is not on the system PATH.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check that the configured endpoint is reachable before enabling the service:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo systemd-run --wait --pipe --collect --service-type=exec \e
    -p EnvironmentFile=-/etc/opentelemetry/jmx-scraper/jmx-scraper.env \e
    -p Environment=JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS=--add-exports=java.security.sasl/com.sun.security.sasl=ALL-UNNAMED \e
    -p DynamicUser=yes -p ProtectHome=yes \e
    /usr/bin/env java -jar /usr/lib/opentelemetry-jmx-scraper/opentelemetry-jmx-scraper.jar \e
    -test -config /etc/opentelemetry/jmx-scraper/config.properties
.fi
.RE
.PP
The command connects, prints "JMX connection test OK" or "JMX connection test ERROR", and exits without scraping or exporting anything.
Run it after every change to config.properties or jmx-scraper.env.
.PP
Running the jar straight from a shell acts differently than the service, and
reports a failure the service would not have. The reason is that credentials
live in jmx-scraper.env, which a shell does not read and which is mode 0640, so
a non-root operator cannot source it. A shell also runs as the invoking user
rather than the service's transient one, so it can reach a relocated
JMX_SCRAPER_CONFIG that the sandboxed service cannot.
.BR systemd-run (1)
reproduces the unit's environment and sandbox instead.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/opentelemetry/jmx-scraper/config.properties
JMX scraper and OTLP exporter configuration. World-readable, and so not a
place for secrets. See SECURITY.
.TP
.I /etc/opentelemetry/jmx-scraper/jmx-scraper.env
Environment overrides for the systemd service, and the place for credentials.
Installed mode 0640.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/opentelemetry-jmx-scraper/opentelemetry-jmx-scraper.jar
The scraper application.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/opentelemetry-jmx-scraper.service
The systemd service unit.
.SH SECURITY
The unit sets DynamicUser=yes, so systemd allocates the service a transient
unprivileged user at every start. The scraper reads a configuration file and
opens outbound connections, so it needs no privileges of its own.
.PP
That is why the two configuration files differ in permissions.
config.properties is world-readable, because the service reads it as its
transient user; restricting it stops the service from reading its own
configuration. jmx-scraper.env is installed mode 0640, because systemd reads it
as root before dropping privileges, so the service's own user cannot read it.
.PP
Put secrets in jmx-scraper.env, never in config.properties. Every scraper
property has an environment variable equivalent \(em otel.jmx.password is
OTEL_JMX_PASSWORD \(em so credentials and OTLP headers can live entirely in
the restricted file.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The scraper logs to the journal, which is the only place its errors are visible:
.PP
.RS
.nf
journalctl -u opentelemetry-jmx-scraper.service -f
.fi
.RE
.PP
A rejected configuration exits 1 and a missing Java runtime exits 127.
The unit sets Restart=on-failure, so systemd restarts the service every five seconds instead of leaving it in the failed state.
.BR systemctl (1)
therefore reports a misconfigured service as activating rather than failed.
.PP
An unreachable JMX endpoint is not a startup failure.
The scraper keeps running, logs "Failed to establish JMX connection", and retries until the endpoint answers.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR java (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd.unit (5),
.BR systemd-run (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
